<p>English builds a question with <i>do</i>: <i>Do you live in Amsterdam?</i> Dutch has no <i>do</i>. The verb itself moves. In a yes-or-no question it comes first. <b>Je</b> after the verb drops <b>-t</b>. <b>U</b>, for a stranger or someone older, never drops it. The same split holds for <b>zijn</b> and <b>hebben</b>: <b>Ben je moe?</b> but <b>Bent u moe?</b> For <b>hebben</b> with <b>u</b>, both <b>hebt u</b> and <b>heeft u</b> are used in the Netherlands.</p>
